==============================================================================
STEP 7   The DIFFERENTIAL test -- immune to a common-mode error
==============================================================================

    A single wrong Upsilon_K, h_z or k shifts every galaxy's B_z by the SAME
    factor.  It cannot create a correlation between the measured B_z and the
    B_z a law predicts galaxy by galaxy.  The DiskMass sample spans a factor of
    ~35 in central surface density, so the laws predict B_z varying across the
    sample, and the slope of that relation is a test with no common-mode floor.

        log10 B_z(observed)  =  c  +  s * log10 B_z(law)

    s = 0 : the vertical field does not track the law's prediction (Newton)
    s = 1 : it tracks it exactly

    law             range predicted           slope s [68%]  resid rms   infl
    rar                       0.390     +1.19 [+0.95,+1.45]      0.163   1.34
    aqual_simple              0.354     +1.31 [+1.05,+1.61]      0.163   1.33
    tensor_aniso              0.027  +17.20 [+13.34,+21.60]      0.166   1.36
    tensor_iso                0.086     +5.56 [+4.27,+7.23]      0.167   1.37

    residual scatter about the fit vs the scatter the nuisance model
    itself produces (0.122 dex): the ratio
    is the inflation applied to sd(s) below, so an under-modelled
    galaxy-to-galaxy term cannot masquerade as significance.
    law              slope s    sd raw   sd inflated   s/sd (inflated)
    rar                1.185     0.314         0.420              2.82
    aqual_simple       1.308     0.353         0.471              2.78
    tensor_aniso      17.203     5.016         6.822              2.52
    tensor_iso         5.556     1.580         2.160              2.57

    Label-control null: the predicted values are SHUFFLED across
    galaxies, destroying the physical pairing but preserving both
    marginals.  A slope the null reaches is not evidence.
    law               true slope            null 2.5-97.5%   p(one-sided)
    rar                    1.185            [-0.85, +0.80]         0.0000
    aqual_simple           1.308            [-0.86, +0.96]         0.0050
    tensor_aniso          17.203          [-12.63, +12.14]         0.0050
    tensor_iso             5.556            [-3.84, +3.83]         0.0075

    Confound audit.  The predictor is driven by surface density, which
    also drives colour and h_z.  Partial slopes with those held:
    law                raw s   | B-K held   | mu0_K held  | h_z/h_R held
    rar                1.180        1.234          1.037           1.122
    aqual_simple       1.305        1.362          1.178           1.240
    tensor_aniso      17.165       17.123         10.959          16.566
    tensor_iso         5.571        5.465          3.272           5.415

    Direct form of the same test, against the raw driver.  Regress
    log10 B_z(observed) on log10 Sigma_0(K band), and compare with the
    slope each law predicts for that same regression.  This needs no
    law-specific predictor and is the least model-dependent version.
    quantity                             d log10 B_z / d log10 Sigma_0
    OBSERVED                  -0.346  68% [-0.416, -0.276]  95% [-0.483, -0.220]
                                             sd inflated x1.35 = 0.173
    Newton predicts                                              0.000
    rar predicts              -0.291  68% [-0.325, -0.254]  95% [-0.357, -0.220]
    aqual_simple predicts     -0.264  68% [-0.296, -0.228]  95% [-0.330, -0.196]
    tensor_aniso predicts     -0.020  68% [-0.022, -0.017]  95% [-0.024, -0.014]
    tensor_iso predicts       -0.055  68% [-0.068, -0.043]  95% [-0.078, -0.035]

    The observed slope is 2.0 sigma from the Newtonian zero.  That is the one place in this
    analysis where the data have real power, because a common-mode error
    in Upsilon_K, h_z or k moves the INTERCEPT, not the slope.

7b  audits of that slope, because it is the only live signal here
    (i)  bootstrap over galaxies       : -0.349  68% [-0.456, -0.221]  95% [-0.561, -0.071]
         fraction of bootstrap resamples with slope >= 0 : 0.0095
         -- the most conservative error estimator here, because it
            absorbs any galaxy-to-galaxy term the nuisance model misses
         leave-one-out range           : [-0.315, -0.204]
    (ii) an instrumental dispersion floor would bias the LOW-sigma end
         upward and could fake a negative slope.  Add a spurious
         quadrature term eps to the observed sigma^2 and refit:
         eps =  0.0 km/s in quadrature -> slope -0.344
         eps =  5.0 km/s in quadrature -> slope -0.337
         eps = 10.0 km/s in quadrature -> slope -0.319
         eps = 15.0 km/s in quadrature -> slope -0.292
         eps = 20.0 km/s in quadrature -> slope -0.260
         DiskMass instrumental sigma is ~17-20 km/s and is removed in
         their fits; even a fully uncorrected 20 km/s residual moves the
         slope by far less than the distance to zero.
    (iii) gas fraction anticorrelates with surface density in real
          spirals, and M_gas is NOT tabulated here.  Impose a trend
          f_gas ~ Sigma_0^(-b) at fixed sample mean and refit:
          d log f_gas/d log Sigma_0 = -0.0 -> observed slope -0.343
          d log f_gas/d log Sigma_0 = -0.2 -> observed slope -0.330
          d log f_gas/d log Sigma_0 = -0.4 -> observed slope -0.311
          d log f_gas/d log Sigma_0 = -0.6 -> observed slope -0.304
          A gas fraction varying by a factor ~6 across the sample shifts
          the slope by well under half the distance to zero, and the
          shift is in the direction that WEAKENS, not creates, it.

    (iv) which inputs vary systematically with Sigma_0 at all?
         corr(log Sigma_0, inclination i_TF          ) = -0.057
         corr(log Sigma_0, sin^2 i                   ) = -0.012
         corr(log Sigma_0, B-K colour                ) = +0.336
         corr(log Sigma_0, log h_R                   ) = -0.332
         corr(log Sigma_0, log h_z                   ) = -0.347
         corr(log Sigma_0, log sigma_LOS_0 (observed)) = +0.679
         The inclination is UNCORRELATED with Sigma_0, so an error in
         alpha -- which enters only through sin^2 i -- cannot fake the
         slope.  h_R and hence h_z do correlate, so the h_z-h_R relation
         is the one input that could.  Perturb its exponent and refit:
         d(log h_z)/d(log h_R) shifted by -0.30 -> slope -0.366
         d(log h_z)/d(log h_R) shifted by -0.15 -> slope -0.352
         d(log h_z)/d(log h_R) shifted by +0.00 -> slope -0.340
         d(log h_z)/d(log h_R) shifted by +0.15 -> slope -0.330
         d(log h_z)/d(log h_R) shifted by +0.30 -> slope -0.316
         d log h_R/d log Sigma_0 = -0.174 in this sample, so an
         error d in the relation's exponent moves the slope by about
         +0.174 d.  Removing the observed slope entirely would need
         d = 1.99, against a published
         exponent of 0.643 -- i.e. the Bershady+2010b relation would have
         to be wrong in SLOPE by that much, not merely in zero point.

==============================================================================
STEP 8   What precision would make this decisive?
==============================================================================

    The amplitude test measures  log B_z = 2 log sigma_z(obs) - log Upsilon_K
    - log h_z - log k + const.  Its width is 0.192 dex, and the dominant
    terms are common-mode, so N galaxies do not help.

    Separations that must be resolved.  B_z(law) here is the AMPLITUDE-based
    value -- the boost in the fitted sigma_LOS_0, which is what the amplitude
    test actually measures -- not the pointwise value at one radius.
    law             log10 B_z(law) amp    separation from Newton  sigma at current
    newton                       0.000                     0.000              0.00
    rar                          0.189                     0.189              0.99
    aqual_simple                 0.190                     0.190              0.99
    tensor_aniso                 0.010                     0.010              0.05
    tensor_iso                   0.001                     0.001              0.01

    Is each law's B_z CONSISTENT with the observed one?  This is the
    question the brief asks, and it is not the same as the table above.
    law             log10 Bz(law)  log10 Bz(obs)  difference   sigma     verdict
    newton                  0.000         -0.146       0.146    0.76  consistent
    rar                     0.189         -0.146       0.335    1.70  consistent
    aqual_simple            0.190         -0.146       0.335    1.71  consistent
    tensor_aniso            0.010         -0.146       0.156    0.81  consistent
    tensor_iso              0.001         -0.146       0.147    0.76  consistent
    Every law is within 2 sigma of the observed amplitude, including
    Newton and including the two that predict a 50 per cent vertical
    boost.  The amplitude does not choose between them.

    To separate the strongest MOND-like prediction from Newton at 3 sigma the
    total budget on log10 B_z must fall below 0.063 dex.
    log B_z = 2 log sigma_z - log Upsilon_K - log h_z - log k + const, so the
    four dominant terms add in quadrature and each must reach about
    0.032 dex (8 per cent).  Term by term, against today:
    term                                   today    needed   factor
    Upsilon_K zero point                   0.154     0.032      4.9x
    alpha = sigma_z/sigma_R                0.078     0.032      2.5x
    h_z relation zero point                0.072     0.032      2.3x
    k, vertical profile shape              0.033     0.032      1.1x
    Concretely that means, simultaneously:
      * Upsilon_K to ~8 per cent ABSOLUTE (an IMF zero point, not a
        relative calibration) -- the hardest of the four;
      * h_z MEASURED, not inferred from h_R: edge-on scale heights for these
        galaxies, or a resolved vertical decomposition;
      * the profile shape k pinned to ~10 per cent, which means resolving the
        tracer population mix -- an old thick component and a young thin one do
        not share a scale height, and the DiskMass sigma_z is a mixture;
      * the velocity ellipsoid alpha measured rather than adopted, which needs
        galaxies at two inclinations or a proper 3-integral model;
      * an independent inclination, so the rotation side stops depending on the
        inverted Tully-Fisher relation.
    None of these is a matter of more galaxies.

    The DIFFERENTIAL slope is the route that does NOT need the zero points.
    Its current width is set by the per-galaxy scatter and the range of the
    predictor.  Scaling sd(s) ~ sigma_gal /(sqrt(N) sd(x)):
      rar            sd(s) = 0.420 (inflated); N for sd(s)=0.15 :    220   N for sd(s)=0.10 :    495
      aqual_simple   sd(s) = 0.471 (inflated); N for sd(s)=0.15 :    277   N for sd(s)=0.10 :    622
      tensor_aniso   sd(s) = 6.822 (inflated); N for sd(s)=0.15 :  57923   N for sd(s)=0.10 : 130326
      tensor_iso     sd(s) = 2.160 (inflated); N for sd(s)=0.15 :   5807   N for sd(s)=0.10 :  13066
    Only the RAR predictor has enough dynamic range across this sample
    for the differential route to be affordable.  For the tensor laws the
    predicted B_z barely varies galaxy to galaxy, so no sample size makes the
    differential test work -- a wider range in surface density is needed, not
    more galaxies at the same surface density.

